      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 Paediatrics and Child Health Division (The Royal Australasian College of Physicians).",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "The aim of this study is to determine the association between waist circumference (WC) and childhood-masked hypertension."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A territory-wide, school-based cohort of 1385 Hong Kong students (672 boys and 713 girls) aged 8-17\u2009years was analysed. The ambulatory blood pressure-monitoring assessment was performed using validated oscillometric recorders (A&D TM-2430 (A&D Inc., Tokyo, Japan)) following American Heart Association's recommendations. Subjects were considered normotensive if their casual blood pressure, 24-h daytime and night-time average systolic blood pressure and diastolic blood pressure (DBP) were <95th percentile. If one or more of the ambulatory blood pressure parameters was ≥95th percentile, subjects would be considered suffering from masked hypertension (MH). Subjects who had three successive casual blood pressure measurements above the 95th percentile were excluded."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "By body mass index, 148 (10.7%) subjects were obese, 182 (13.1%) overweight and 359 (25.9%) having larger WC (≥85th percentiles). MH was diagnosed in 217 subjects (15.7%). Subjects with larger WC or obesity were significantly associated with higher 24-h daytime and night-time systolic blood pressure (≥95th percentile) (odds ratios from 1.84 to 2.09 and from 2.07 to 3.54 for larger WC and obese respectively, all P\u2009<\u20090.05) as well as 24-h DBP for larger WC (odds ratio\u2009=\u20092, P\u2009=\u20090.015) than normal subjects adjusted by sex, age and height."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Waist circumference and body mass index are independent risk factors of childhood and adolescent MH. WC appears a significant associated factor of elevated 24-h DBP in children aged 8-17\u2009years."
          }
        ]
      },
